\section{Matching modulo AC}




\subsection{A note on approaches}
For AC-rules, do we want to allow rules of the form $e_1 + \ldots + e_n \rightarrow d_1 + \ldots + d_k$ or restrict to simply $e_1 + \ldots + e_n \rightarrow e$? The former is closer to usual presentations of Buchberger's algorithm, while the latter is more in the spirit of E-node rules. We will refer to these as the \emph{side-table} and \emph{integrated} approaches. Both are valid, but there is a subtlety in the integrated approach that might be missed, while the side-table approach makes it much more obvious.

\subsection{EMT(AC)s via a side-table, briefly}
With the side-table approach, we think of the set of AC rules $e_1 + \ldots + e_n \rightarrow d_1 + \ldots + d_n$ as being a separate thing entirely to the E-graph.

On insertion, we don't add any E-node rules whenever the function symbol is an AC symbol +, but generate a new E-class name $e$ and add the AC-rule $e_1 + \ldots + e_i \rightarrow e$ when we insert the flattened term $e_1 + \ldots + e_i$.

To compute the AC-closure, we take every pair of two AC-rules $+(R) \rightarrow +(S)$ and $+(T) \rightarrow +(U)$, where $R, S, T, U$ are multisets of E-classes. Then we can define, as usual,
\begin{itemize}
\item The overlap: $\min(R, T)$
\item The critical term: $\max(R, T) = R + T - \min(R, T)$
\item The critical pair: $c_1 = \max(R, T) - R + S$ and $c_2 = \max(R, T) - T + U$
\end{itemize}

When the overlap is not the empty multiset, then we take the critical pair and try to rewrite both sides by existing rules until it is minimal. If it is still non-trivial, we \emph{orient} it, according to some ordering on flat AC-terms. A common choice for Buchberger's algorithm is \emph{grevlex} order, graded reverse-lexicographic, which is used in our implementation.

After orienting the reduced rule, we reduce every other rule with respect to it, and add it to the set of AC rules.

The final ingredient is the `combination rule'. If we were ever to have a reduced rule $e_1 \rightarrow e_2$, with 1-term sums on either side, we should instead treat this as a union-find rule, and correspondingly propagate its consequences into the union-find and E-node rules. And vice versa, whenever the union-find rules are updated, we need to update any AC-rules according to the new ordering on atoms induced by the union-find rule.

\subsection{Integrated EMT(AC)s, briefly}
Unlike the side-table approach, here we will only allow AC rules with an atomic right-hand-side, thus making them of the same form as E-node rules.

As before, we can now form critical pairs $c_1, c_2$ for rules with non-empty overlap. We proceed as above and reduce the critical terms as much as possible by existing rules. Then we need to create a new E-class id $e$ and insert the rules $c_{1\textrm{reduced}} \rightarrow e$ and $c_{2\textrm{reduced}} \rightarrow e$.

One subtlety is that here we want to reduce \emph{existing} rules by the new one. This interreduction step is a standard step in Buchberger's algorithm, but may not be obvious in the `integrated' viewpoint, as it is a form of destructive rewriting on the E-graph.

In the integrated viewpoint, this amounts to shrinking E-nodes when they can `factor' through another node. For instance, if we have E-nodes $a + b + c$ in class $e_1$ and $b + c$ in class $e_2$, we ought to destructively rewrite the first to $a + e_2$. Just resolving critical pairs will lead to $e_1$ containing both $a + b + c$ and $a + e_2$, which is correct as far as dealing with critical pairs but redundant.

This interreduction shrinks the size of the E-graph, so it is important for performance in general and \emph{required} to properly handle cyclic E-graphs.

\subsection{Comparing the approaches}
Despite the fact that side-table rules of the form $e_1 + e_2 + \ldots \rightarrow e_3 + e_4 + \ldots$ are less natural from an E-graph perspective than integrated rules of the form $e_1 + e_2 + \ldots \rightarrow e$, the side-table approach has a key advantage.

In the integrated approach, an equation $e_1 + e_2 + \ldots = e_3 + e_4 + \ldots$ is oriented into \emph{two} AC-rules $e_1 + e_2 + \ldots \rightarrow e$ and $e_3 + e_4 + \ldots \rightarrow e$. When computing critical pairs, we have to find critical pairs between both new left-hand sides. On the other hand, the side table approach orients this equation to either $e_1 + e_2 + \ldots \rightarrow e_3 + e_4 + \ldots$ or $e_3 + e_4 + \ldots \rightarrow e_1 + e_2 + \ldots$, in effect `subsuming' the term on the right by the rewrite, so we don't need to compute critical pairs between it and other terms. 

This subsumption is facilitated by an ordering on (flat) AC-terms, which is required for the side-table approach. Generating new constants in the integrated approach is, in effect, a very special term ordering: each new constant generated can be freely put below any terms that it appears on the right of. The naturality of this approach obscurs the fact that this ordering even exists. The AC-closure algorithm is a vairant of Buchberger's algorithm, and it has been observed that the performance of Buchberger's algorithm depends quite critically on the ordering used \cite[Chapter~2, section~10, subsection \textit{Complexity~issues}]{cox_ideals_2015}.

We will prove in section \ref{equivres} the equivalence of the approaches in that they both terminate with a confluent rewriting system. However, the exact rewrite system generated is sensitive to the ordering on terms used to generate it, and in general we expect the side-table approach to be more flexible and efficient than the integrated approach, at the expense of some extra conceptual and implementation complexity.

Prior approaches to congruence closure modulo AC \cite{bachmair_abstract_2000, tiwari_decision_2000, kapur_modular_2021} have entirely focused on what we refer to as the side-table approach.

\section{EMT(AC)s, formally}
\begin{definition}
	An integrated EMT(AC) consists of the set $C$ and the relation $\rightarrow_U$ and $\rightarrow_F$ of an E-graph together with a relation $\rightarrow_{AC} \subseteq \mathcal{M_\textrm{fin}}(C) \times C$, where $\mathcal{M_\textrm{fin}}$ is the set of finite multisets over $C$.
\end{definition}

The sense in which this is integrated is that $\rightarrow_{AC}$ can be thought of as a special subset of $\rightarrow_F$.

\begin{definition}
	A side-table EMT(AC) consists of the set $C$ and the relation $\rightarrow_U$ and $\rightarrow_F$ of an E-graph together with a relation $\rightarrow_{AC} \subseteq \mathcal{M_\textrm{fin}}(C) \times \mathcal{M_\textrm{fin}}(C)$
\end{definition}

\subsection{Canonization}
First, we describe AC-canonization:
\begin{algorithm}
	Input: an EMT $(C, \rightarrow_U, \rightarrow_F, \rightarrow_{AC})$ and an AC-term $t$.
	
	Procedure: repeat either of the following steps until neither is applicable:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item If there is a rule $s \rightarrow_{AC} e_i$ such that $s$ is an AC-subterm of $t$, replace $t$ with $t - s + e_i$ ($t - s$ is defined as $s$ is an AC-subterm of $t$).
			\item If there is a rule $e_j \rightarrow_U e_i$ such that $e_j$ appears in $t$, replace all copies of $s_j$ in $t$ with $e_i$.
		\end{enumerate}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{The algorithm for integrated EMT(AC) closure}
Using canonization we can describe the integrated EMT(AC) variant:

\begin{algorithm}
	Input: an EMT $(C, \rightarrow_U, \rightarrow_F, \rightarrow_{AC})$ and a set $R$ of AC-equations.
	
	Procedure: while $R$ is not empty:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item Remove $t_1 = t_2$ from $R$.
			\item AC-canonize both $t_1$ and $t_2$. Call the canonized forms $t_1'$ and $t_2'$.
			\item If $t_1'$ and $t_2'$ are single E-classes $e_1$ and $e_2$, then:
				\begin{enumerate}
					\item If $e_1 \neq e_2$, then:
						\begin{enumerate}
							\item Add $e_1 \rightarrow e_2$ to $\rightarrow_U$ and recompute congruence closure on $\rightarrow_U$ and $\rightarrow_F$.
							\item For any AC-rule $s \rightarrow_{AC} e_i$ where one of the E-classes in $s$ or $e_i$ has a successor in the union find, remove that rule from $\rightarrow_{AC}$ and add $s = e_i$ to $R$.
						\end{enumerate}
					\item Otherwise, since $e_1 = e_2$, go to the top of the loop.
				\end{enumerate}
			\item Otherwise:
				\begin{itemize}
					\item Extend $C$ to $C \cup \{e_n\}$ for a new constant $e_n$
					\item Add the rules $t_1' \rightarrow_{AC} e_n$ and $t_2' \rightarrow_{AC} e_n$ to $\rightarrow_{AC}$.
					\item For every rule except the two just added, if $t_1'$ or $t_2'$ is a subterm of the left-hand-side, remove that rule $s \rightarrow_{AC} e_i$ from $\rightarrow_{AC}$ and add $s = e_i$ to $R$.
					\item For every rule $t_l \rightarrow_{AC} t_r$ except the two rules just added, if $t_l$ has $t_1'$ or $t_2'$ as an AC-subterm, remove it from $\rightarrow_{AC}$ and add $t_l = t_r$ to $R$.
					\item For every rule except the two just added, compute the critical pairs between that rule and both the new rules, and add the two equations $c_{1a} = c_{1b}$ and $c_{2a} = c_{2b}$ to $R$, where $c_{1a}$ and $c_{1b}$ are the terms of the critical pair between the old rule and the first new rule, and likewise $c_{2a}$ and $c_{2b}$ for the second new rule.
				\end{itemize}
		\end{enumerate}
\end{algorithm}

This is a little complex, but can be seen as iteratively resolving critical pairs between the various sets of rewrite rules in the EMT. Congruence closure is be run whenever a new nontrivial equation of the form $e_i = e_j$ is discovered. Rules in $\rightarrow_{AC}$ can be invalidated if their left-hand-side occurs in a critical pair with some newly-added rule, so they are moved back into $R$ for further processing.

\subsection{The algorithm for side-table EMT(AC) closure}
The algorithm for the side-table variant is structurally similar.

\begin{algorithm}
	Input: an EMT $(C, \rightarrow_U, \rightarrow_F, \rightarrow_{AC})$ and a set $R$ of AC-equations.
	
	Procedure: while $R$ is not empty:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item Remove $t_1 = t_2$ from $R$.
			\item AC-canonize both $t_1$ and $t_2$. Call the canonized forms $t_1'$ and $t_2'$.
			\item If $t_1'$ and $t_2'$ are single E-classes $e_1$ and $e_2$, then:
				\begin{enumerate}
					\item If $e_1 \neq e_2$, then:
						\begin{enumerate}
							\item Add $e_1 \rightarrow e_2$ to $\rightarrow_U$ and recompute congruence closure on $\rightarrow_U$ and $\rightarrow_F$.
							\item For any AC-rule $s \rightarrow_{AC} e_i$ where one of the E-classes in $s$ or $e_i$ has a successor in the union find, remove that rule from $\rightarrow_{AC}$ and add $s = e_i$ to $R$.
						\end{enumerate}
					\item Otherwise, since $e_1 = e_2$, go to the top of the loop.
				\end{enumerate}
			\item Otherwise:
				\begin{itemize}
					\item Compare the terms $t_1'$ and $t_2'$ according to some ordering, and call the smaller $t_a$ and the larger $t_b$.
					\item Add the rule $t_b \rightarrow_{AC} t_a$ to $\rightarrow_{AC}$.
					\item For every rule $t_l \rightarrow_{AC} t_r$ except the rule just added, if $t_l$ has $t_b$ as an AC-subterm, remove it from $\rightarrow_{AC}$ and add $t_l = t_r$ to $R$.
					\item For every rule except the one just added, compute the critical pair between that rule and the new rule, and add the equation $c_a = c_b$ to $R$, where $c_a$ and $c_b$ are the terms of the critical pair.
				\end{itemize}
		\end{enumerate}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Correctness and partial equivalence of the algorithms} \label{equivres}
We will prove termination and correctness for the side-table algorithm first, and then show how to relate the integrated algorithm to the side-table one to re-use this proof.

Define the \emph{Dickson ordering} on multisets as the partial ordering $s \ge t$ if the multiplicity of every element in $s$ is $\ge$ the multiplicity of the same element in $t$. This is the same as saying that $s$ has $t$ as an AC-subterm.

\begin{lemma}
	At each iteration of the algorithm, there are no rules in $\rightarrow_{AC}$ with left-hand-sides that are related by $\ge$ in the Dickson ordering.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
	If $s \ge t$, then $s$ has $t$ as an AC-subterm. Before each iteration, this invariant holds for every pair of existing rules. When a new rule is added in step 4, any rule which would be greater than the new left-hand-side is removed and added to $R$, so this invariant is restored.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
	(Stability) When a rule $t_l \rightarrow_{AC} t_r$ is removed, any further rule with a left-hand-side $s$ which would satisfy $s \ge t_l$ will satisfy this with one of the remaining rules.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
	Whenever we remove a rule with left-hand-side $t_l$, this is because we determined that $t_l \ge t$ for some new left-hand-side $t$. So any $s$ for which $s \ge t_l$ would then satisfy $s \ge t_l \ge t$ with the remaining set of rules that include $t$ on the left of the new rule.
\end{proof}

Therefore, the set of left-hand-sides in $\rightarrow_{AC}$ is a set of elements in $\mathcal{M}_\textrm{fin}(C)$, none greater than any other in the Dickson ordering. Each iteration, we either
	\begin{itemize}
		\item Add a new rewrite to $\rightarrow_U$, and do some other changes
		\item Add a new element to this set of left-hand-sides, possibly removing some existing elements, and do some other changes
		\item Remove one equation from $R$.
	\end{itemize}
	
Since $C$ is fixed, only finitely many changes to $\rightarrow_U$ are possible, since at most we can equate every element in $C$ in the union-find.

Once the algorithm reaches the point it doesn't alter $\rightarrow_U$	any further, then we look at how it changes the set of left-hand sides of rules in $\rightarrow_AC$. Any new element must not just be not greater than any existing element, but also any past ones, by the stability lemma. Therefore, we can consider the entire set of previous and current elements, and this set only grows.

By Dickson's lemma \cites{goos_congruence_2000}, there can't be an infinite set of elements in $\mathcal{M}_\textrm{fin}(C)$ over a finite set $C$ that are pairwise incomparable. Therefore, the process of adding rules must terminate at some point.

Once adding rules stops, the remaining equations in $R$ will be removed and the algorithm terminates.

Correctness is fairly straightforward: critical pairs are added to $R$ and eventually resolved. Assuming that the ordering on terms includes the Dickson ordering, then orienting a critical pair $t = s$ from $R$ into $t \rightarrow s$ means that 

